 8. 🏈 1 fun thing: NFL&#x27;s French connection 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A custom-built Arc de Triomphe entrance portal for player introductions at yesterday's Steelers-Saints game at the Stade de France in Saint-Denis near Paris. Photo: Franck Fife/AFP via Getty Images The French gave us the Statue of Liberty , crème brûlée and haute couture. Now we're giving them NFL football, Bill Kole (your Axios AM copy editor) writes from his base in Paris. 

 Why it matters: The NFL has held games in Australia, Brazil, Germany, Ireland, Spain and the U.K. — and yesterday's showdown between the Pittsburgh Steelers and New Orleans Saints was the league's first in France as it seeks to grow international interest . Security was tight for the sold-out game ( final score : Steelers 16, Saints 10) at the 80,000-seat Stade de France, but it was a welcome distraction after weeks of violent protests and widespread social unrest across France.

 ⚜️ The backstory: New Orleans, established by the French in 1718 and rich in French architecture, food and culture, was the sentimental favorite.

 💭 Bill's thought bubble: I'm a lifelong New England Patriots fan — I grew up in Foxborough, Mass., home to Gillette Stadium — so I had no skin in this game.

 🌭 The food at the Stade de France was predictably fab, though — even Le Hot-Dog. More on the game . 

 
 
